What is a Network Policy?

A NetworkPolicy in Kubernetes is used to control traffic flow between Pods, Namespaces, and external networks.

It acts like a firewall for Pods


	Simple Definition


NetworkPolicy = Security rules for Pod-to-Pod communication


Why Do We Use Network Policies?

Without NetworkPolicy:

	All pods can talk to each other (default open network)

With NetworkPolicy:

	Restrict traffic (Zero Trust security)

	Protect sensitive apps (DB, APIs)

	Block unauthorized access


Types of Traffic Control
	
	Type		Description
	
	Ingress		Incoming traffic to Pod
	
	Egress		Outgoing traffic from Pod

Deny All Traffic


	apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
	kind: NetworkPolicy
	metadata:
	  name: deny-all
	  namespace: default
	spec:
	  podSelector: {}   # applies to all pods
	  policyTypes:
	  - Ingress
	  - Egress

	 Result:

	No pod can send/receive traffic



Example 3: Allow Traffic on Specific Port


apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-http
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web

  ingress:
  - ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80



Only HTTP traffic allowed


Important Commands



Create Policy

kubectl apply -f network-policy.yaml


Get Policies

kubectl get networkpolicies


or

kubectl get netpol


Describe Policy

kubectl describe netpol <name>

Delete Policy

kubectl delete netpol <name>

Test Connectivity

kubectl exec -it <pod> -- curl <target-pod-ip>

	Used to verify allow/deny rules

Key Concepts
	podSelector

Selects target pods
	namespaceSelector

Filters namespaces

ipBlock

Allow/deny external IPs
	Important Note 

	NetworkPolicy works only if:


Your cluster uses a network plugin that supports it
Calico ✅
Cilium ✅


Flannel ❌ (basic mode)


🔹 Advantages

✔ Strong security (Zero Trust)

✔ Fine-grained control

✔ Protects internal services



🔹 Limitations

❌ Requires supported CNI
❌ Complex for large setups
❌ Default allow until policy applied
🔹 Real DevOps Scenario 



👉 Microservices app:

Frontend → Backend ✅
Backend → Database ✅

Frontend → Database ❌

👉 Achieved using NetworkPolicy


Example 1: Deny All Traffic

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}   # applies to all pods
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress
  - Egress

👉 Result:

No pod can send/receive traffic

🔹 Example 2: Allow Only Specific App (Frontend → Backend)

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-frontend
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend

  policyTypes:
  - Ingress

  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: frontend

Only frontend pods can access backend

🔹 Example 3: Allow Traffic on Specific Port

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-http
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web

  ingress:
  - ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80

Only HTTP traffic allowed

🔹 Example 4: Allow Namespace-Based Access

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-namespace
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: db

  ingress:
  - from:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          name: prod

Only pods from prod namespace can access DB

🔹 Example 5: Egress Policy (Allow External Access)

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-external
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: app

  policyTypes:
  - Egress

  egress:
  - to:
    - ipBlock:
        cidr: 0.0.0.0/0

Allows outgoing traffic to internet


